00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.640
So before we dive into the meat
of how cost reporting works

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.990
on Google Cloud, I want to
do a little introductory trip

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:14.720
into cost management
in the cloud

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.690
with a company you've probably
heard of before, Waze.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.690
If you live in the Bay
Area, you might have even

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.000
used Waze to get here.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.870
Waze was founded in 2008.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.070
It was called FreeMap
Israel, I believe.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.370
And it was acquired
by Google in 2013.

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:29.987
So now it's a Google company.

00:01:29.987 --> 00:01:32.570
And obviously, they run part of
their infrastructure on Google

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.250
Cloud Platform,
or else I wouldn't

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:36.219
be including them in this talk.

00:01:36.219 --> 00:01:38.510
One of the things they do,
which you are familiar with,

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:40.310
is their traffic application.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:42.657
They help you navigate and
get to where you need to go.

00:01:42.657 --> 00:01:44.240
They find the best
route for you based

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.120
on help from other drivers.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.560
And this is actually
a map of where

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.330
Phill and I work in Seattle.

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:53.300
And it's showing you three
routes from Downtown Seattle

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.070
to Fremont.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:55.820
Now, two of the routes,
I don't recommend.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:57.736
But the fastest route
does seem like the route

00:01:57.736 --> 00:02:01.252
I would take to get from the
Hilton downtown to Fremont.

00:02:01.252 --> 00:02:02.960
And right where that
Google Cloud logo is

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.540
is Google in Fremont.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.539
And you can kind of see
what Waze is doing here.

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:12.430
It has traffic notifications
based on drivers.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.720
So this is all coming
in from drivers' apps.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.570
There's heavy traffic
on the 520 bridge.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.778
There's basically heavy
traffic everywhere in Seattle

00:02:19.778 --> 00:02:20.900
this morning at 9:00 AM.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.080
That's pretty standard.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.845
There's also other events,
like these pink barricades

00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:28.460
that are showing where
drivers are reporting roads

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:29.330
are shut down.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.550
There's a policeman,
apparently, in Interbay.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.560
And there's construction
workers downtown.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.030
So the important
thing about this

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:36.920
is, Waze is doing
this, as you know,

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:39.410
all over the world
for millions of users.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:42.560
They're in 185 countries.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.020
And they have 500,000
maps contributors.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:48.950
And we all know that route
navigation and route finding

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:51.297
is already a computationally
hard problem.

00:02:51.297 --> 00:02:53.130
And Waze is doing a lot
more than just that.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:55.190
So it's doing this
for millions of users

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:57.020
around the world in real time.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:58.926
It's also doing
anomaly detection.

00:02:58.926 --> 00:03:00.800
It's reporting what it
thinks might be safety

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.600
incidents to local authorities.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.520
It's doing sentiment
analysis on users

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:08.390
to make sure that their
experience on the Waze app

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:09.260
is good.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:11.524
It's taking all of those
map contributions in.

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:13.190
And it's doing this
in every major city.

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:15.481
And you can kind of see--
what I love about this video,

00:03:15.481 --> 00:03:17.570
and you can see a series
of these on YouTube,

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:20.510
is how Waze is
living and breathing

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:23.060
as it's serving its customers.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:27.950
And in particular, all of
its services on this chart,

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:31.130
and on this slide and elsewhere,
are spinning up and spinning

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:33.090
down with Waze customer demand.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:35.630
So in the morning, when
Waze customers are driving,

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:37.490
Waze is spinning up
more compute resources.

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:39.080
There's more BigQuery activity.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:43.580
There's more big data
activity-- storage and database,

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:45.290
management, networking activity.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:48.144
And actually, in a chart
later on in this presentation,

00:03:48.144 --> 00:03:49.310
you'll see what's happening.

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:52.134
And this a really cool aspect
of all of these services

00:03:52.134 --> 00:03:54.050
and of being in the
cloud, is that they're all

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:55.430
available on demand.

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:58.100
And they literally
breathe in and out

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:02.209
with the demand on
the infrastructure.

00:04:02.209 --> 00:04:04.500
So that's something I just
want to talk about and frame

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.041
this talk a little
bit before we dive

00:04:06.041 --> 00:04:07.350
into the cost reporting tools.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:08.850
It is one of the
things you probably

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:12.944
hear a lot about moving onto the
cloud from a legacy provider,

00:04:12.944 --> 00:04:14.610
or operating on various
cloud providers,

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:17.250
is the ability to trade
capital expenditures

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:19.079
for operational expenditures.

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:22.019
So here's just an illustrative
oversimplification-- cost

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:24.940
on the y-axis and
time on the x-axis.

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:27.000
And this might be a
hypothetical application,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.291
and you're forecasting the
demand that application will

00:04:29.291 --> 00:04:30.630
have on your infrastructure.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:33.925
And in the
traditional world, you

00:04:33.925 --> 00:04:35.550
would have to make
sure you plan ahead,

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.490
and buy that
infrastructure, and procure

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:38.490
the hardware in advance.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.940
And this timeline could be
anywhere between days to weeks.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:42.630
And usually, it's
more like months--

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:45.450
three months to six months to
get the data center in place,

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:48.150
to get the capacity you need
to run a new application,

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.714
to get the capacity you need
to launch in a new region,

00:04:50.714 --> 00:04:52.380
to launch your business
internationally,

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.130
to serve international
customers.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:58.050
Now, in the worst-case
scenario, you get it wrong.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:01.080
And your demand exceeds
what you planned for.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:04.320
And this either results in
unserved demand or unhappy

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:06.340
customers.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:09.570
And so that's usually why I try
to over-provision to make sure

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:10.362
you don't hit that.

00:05:10.362 --> 00:05:12.111
And then it's a balancing
act, because you

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:13.830
don't want to
over-provision too much,

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:15.570
because in the
best-case scenario,

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:18.910
you have unused capacity.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:20.802
So the obvious immediate
benefit of moving

00:05:20.802 --> 00:05:23.010
from capital expenditures
to operational expenditures

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:26.420
is, this unused capacity
becomes unused capacity savings.

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:28.170
So that's capacity you
didn't have to use,

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:29.550
you didn't have to spin up.

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.860
And all of those services
on the Google Cloud Platform

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:35.925
slide that Waze uses don't
incur costs for Waze.

00:05:35.925 --> 00:05:38.550
But there's another value that
I want you to think hard about--

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:41.520
and sometimes, this gets lost
in some discussions about cost

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.830
monitoring and
cost forecasting--

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.150
is the ability to
meet surprise demand.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:50.910
So this could be, very
simply, a social media app

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:52.560
in the early 2000s
scales really fast

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.726
and shuts down because it
wasn't able to meet demand

00:05:54.726 --> 00:05:56.370
in its data centers.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:58.440
Or you're delivering
traffic more slowly,

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:00.786
and it's just a bad
customer experience.

00:06:00.786 --> 00:06:02.160
But another way
to think about it

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.500
is also, what are the projects
that your company's not

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.699
doing because your
employees are stymied?

00:06:07.699 --> 00:06:09.240
They realize it's
going to take three

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.880
months to get a project off the
ground, to even run a pilot.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:13.470
And so they don't bother asking.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.760
We all work in
knowledge economies.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.640
We have very important
employees who

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:21.630
might be the most valuable thing
that we have in our companies.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:25.290
And whenever we slow down the
work that they want to do,

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:27.620
that has really drastic
implications for our business.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:30.120
So one of my colleagues,
Matt Leonard,

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:33.000
gave a talk with Lucas Carlson
at the Broad Institute.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:36.804
And the Broad was the first
to sequence the human genome.

00:06:36.804 --> 00:06:38.220
And if I recall
correctly, they've

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:41.160
sequenced 100,000
since they started.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:43.400
And you can imagine,
if there's any delay,

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.900
if there's a significant delay
when researchers can actually

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:50.540
get results, that impacts not
just the Broad, but actually--

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:53.310
I know this is kind of
over-emphasizing, but mankind.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:57.630
The time to science
is very long.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:59.910
So some key questions I just
want to put in your head,

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:01.576
and you can ask
yourself these questions

00:07:01.576 --> 00:07:05.580
as you go through the demo
with Phill, are these three.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.710
So how much does my app cost?

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:09.390
What are my cost trends?

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:11.280
And what are the
drivers of those trends?

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:12.495
And what I like about
these questions is,

00:07:12.495 --> 00:07:13.620
they're kind of fractal.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:15.970
They apply to any level
of the organization.

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:19.045
And you'll find that your CIO
or CTO is asking the question.

00:07:19.045 --> 00:07:21.420
And you'll also find that the
developer actually creating

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:23.253
the application or
operating the application

00:07:23.253 --> 00:07:25.620
needs to be able to answer
these questions as well

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.420
in very fine detail.

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:33.300
So we can give you at-a-glance
answers to all three

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:36.150
of these questions, even for
one of our biggest customers.

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:40.230
So I want to illustrate
this with Waze Carpool.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:43.140
Waze Carpool launched, I
think in California last year,

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:45.550
and then in Washington
earlier this year.

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:48.930
And it allows drivers
to pair up with riders

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:52.290
and for a nominal compensation
to be exchanged between the two

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:55.834
so that you can carpool
effectively to work,

00:07:55.834 --> 00:07:57.250
spend time with
people you enjoy--

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:59.610
so you can set preferences
on who you drive with--

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:05.070
get there faster in the HOV
lane, and reduce commute costs.

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:08.130
So this is what Waze costs
looked like in our reports

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:11.247
dashboard earlier this year.

00:08:11.247 --> 00:08:13.080
This is kind of a nice
view, because it also

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:15.454
shows you a general pattern
with what cloud costs usually

00:08:15.454 --> 00:08:17.130
look like, which is
compute costs tend

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:19.830
to be the large cost,
and that makes sense.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:22.970
And there's cyclicality
and periodicity your costs.

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:25.170
It may correspond to
daily cyclicality--

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:29.280
so Waze has that with morning
and evening commutes--

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.210
or seasonal cyclicality--
retail periods, other things

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:34.510
like that.

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:36.870
But in general, there's a
longer-term secular trend

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:39.690
of those costs that also
keeps the cloud administrator

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:41.850
confident that their
environment isn't running out

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:44.964
of control, that they
don't have runaway costs.

00:08:44.964 --> 00:08:46.380
But just at a
glance, you can see,

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:48.184
something happened
earlier last year

00:08:48.184 --> 00:08:49.350
where there was a big spike.

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:51.120
I presume Waze
investigated that,

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:53.550
because that doesn't
look like it was normal.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:55.890
Maybe a few months ago,
there was a smaller spike--

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:57.000
may or may not have been normal.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:58.291
And that's worth investigating.

00:08:58.291 --> 00:09:00.540
So just at a
glance, Waze can log

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:02.730
in to the cloud console
on GCP and see what's

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:04.170
going on with their service.

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:07.710
And again, a service that serves
over 100 million users, where

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:11.120
they've driven 22 and
1/2 billion kilometers,

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:14.380
is all represented by the
infrastructure on this chart.

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:17.220
So then they launched
Waze Carpool.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:19.590
And this was the trend after
they launched Waze Carpool.

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:22.697
And so immediately, we've
answered, at a high level,

00:09:22.697 --> 00:09:23.530
the three questions.

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:24.972
What's my spending level?

00:09:24.972 --> 00:09:27.180
So you have the spending
level at any moment of time.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:27.889
What's the trend?

00:09:27.889 --> 00:09:29.721
And you have this
short-term trend, but also

00:09:29.721 --> 00:09:31.710
the long-term trend,
which is more interesting.

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:32.520
One of the questions
I would want

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:34.095
to ask myself
launching a new service

00:09:34.095 --> 00:09:36.780
is, is the cost growth good?

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:39.300
Is it growing sublinearly
with customer demand,

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:41.160
is it in control,
or is something

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.470
happening where our costs
are spiraling out of control

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:46.500
compared to the
demand we expected?

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:49.290
And this shows a nice story
of what the secular growth was

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.100
before they launched
this major new feature

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:53.310
and what it looked like after
the launched the feature.

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:54.870
And you can also
see that nothing big

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:59.594
changed about their makeup,
at least at a glance, here.

00:09:59.594 --> 00:10:02.010
Compute costs still seem to
be the same fraction, BigQuery

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:04.540
analysis costs, cloud
storage, and so forth.

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:08.190
So just this visual
understanding of cloud costs

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:11.190
is very powerful, because the
visual explanation is just

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:13.630
a really good way to communicate
the structure of costs.

00:10:16.206 --> 00:10:17.580
So I just want to
do one footnote

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.430
before we head into how
to track cloud costs,

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:22.960
because there's really
two ways to go about it.

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:25.320
One way is tops-down.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:26.910
You could use the
resource hierarchy

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:28.482
and put resources in a project.

00:10:28.482 --> 00:10:29.940
Everything on Google
Cloud Platform

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:31.880
is assigned to a project.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:35.310
And you can set up an
organization, folders

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:38.610
under that organization,
projects under each folder,

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:40.380
and then resources
within a project.

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:41.874
And users operate
within projects.

00:10:41.874 --> 00:10:44.290
They have permissions to create
resources within projects.

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:46.050
And so if you've created
a nice project hierarchy,

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:47.640
and you've thought
well about it,

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:50.370
you could have a project that
corresponds to an application.

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:51.745
And then all of
the costs in that

00:10:51.745 --> 00:10:53.640
project are application costs.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:56.689
Or you could have a project that
captures all of an environment,

00:10:56.689 --> 00:10:57.730
like the dev environment.

00:10:57.730 --> 00:11:00.271
So all the costs in that project
would be the dev environment

00:11:00.271 --> 00:11:02.260
costs, and so forth.

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:05.250
The other way to track costs
is bottoms-up through labels.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:07.390
And labels apply to resources.

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:09.900
So you can ask your
developers or set up

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:11.550
tools that automatically
attach labels

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:13.240
to resources that are created.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:17.670
And then you can get, basically,
matrix allocation of costs.

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:19.730
You might say, OK,
within project A,

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:23.380
I know my costs are x in total.

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:28.320
But I also know that the
environment prod in project A

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:30.150
costs some fraction of x.

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:32.950
Environment dev costs some
other fraction, and so on.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:35.066
And that's how you
can use labels.

00:11:35.066 --> 00:11:36.940
You can also attach
other important metadata,

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:39.559
like who's the owner of the
resource, what's the cost

00:11:39.559 --> 00:11:40.600
center, things like that.

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:43.650
So these are two things to think
about as you're-- no matter how

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:46.233
you want to monitor and manage
costs in Google Cloud Platform.

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:52.530
So we're going to focus a lot
of today on billing reports.

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:55.110
But I just want to make
you aware of a couple

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:58.120
of other major features
that might be useful to you

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:00.700
in monitoring and
managing cloud costs.

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:02.370
One is Billing Export.

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:04.110
The other is Budgets and Alerts.

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:06.870
And the last one, which
we'll do an in-depth demo on,

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:08.490
is Billing Reports.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:12.030
So Billing Export is
really where you would just

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:17.250
dive in, write detailed queries,
and answer customized questions

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:18.780
about your cloud costs.

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:21.210
All of the labels go
into Billing Export.

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:26.160
You see what service was used,
what SKU was use, details

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:29.600
about the project and the SKU.

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:32.640
And over time, we'll be adding
more and more information

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:33.665
to BigQuery Export.

00:12:33.665 --> 00:12:35.040
And what's cool
about this is you

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:36.873
can connect to other
tools like Data Studio.

00:12:36.873 --> 00:12:39.327
There's another talk we gave
explaining how to do that.

00:12:39.327 --> 00:12:41.910
And so this really allows you
to answer fine-grained questions

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:44.235
about cloud costs.

00:12:44.235 --> 00:12:46.110
The next feature we
have, Budgets and Alerts,

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:48.030
is really worth
taking a look at.

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:50.310
You can set budgets at the
billing account or project

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:53.280
level and then set
thresholds for when

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:56.070
you want to be notified
about that budget.

00:12:56.070 --> 00:12:59.550
And the notification can happen
via email or programmatically

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:00.330
via Pub/Sub.

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:02.340
So email is great for
cloud administrators who

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:04.673
just want to be aware of
things and may not be operating

00:13:04.673 --> 00:13:06.710
a large-scale operation.

00:13:06.710 --> 00:13:08.460
But Pub/Sub and
programmatic notifications

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:09.720
are especially cool
if you're running

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:12.390
a really large application,
or if you're technically savvy

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:14.820
and can set up really
simple responses

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:18.930
to those notifications, like,
oh, if project A exceeds 90%,

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:21.160
please start
throttling project A.

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:23.730
And that unleashes
very cool solutions

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:24.750
for your organization.

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:27.210
And Matt and Lucas
Carlson from the Broad

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:29.300
give a very cool talk
about that earlier.

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:32.282
It will be on YouTube.

00:13:32.282 --> 00:13:33.740
So now, I'll turn
it over to Phill.

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:35.531
And he'll walk you
through Billing Reports.

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:39.550
PHILL COLETTI: So now,
we're going to dive

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:42.470
into the Billing Reports tool
in a little bit more detail.

00:13:42.470 --> 00:13:43.900
And we'll do that
through the lens

00:13:43.900 --> 00:13:46.150
of our hypothetical
enterprise, Acme Company.

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:50.560
And they want to answer
the same questions

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:52.450
as most other IT organizations.

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:53.530
What are my costs?

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:55.120
What are my cost trends?

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:56.706
And what are my cost drivers?

00:13:56.706 --> 00:13:58.330
But before we actually
look at the tool

00:13:58.330 --> 00:14:01.000
in too much great detail,
a quick show of hands--

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.310
who here has used Google Cloud
Billing Reports in the Cloud

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:04.810
Console?

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:09.420
Looks like maybe 30%.

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:13.080
OK, so for some of you, you
may have already seen this tool

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:13.950
and used it.

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:16.890
For others of you, it may be
familiar, given some other BI

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:18.792
tools that you've used.

00:14:18.792 --> 00:14:21.000
And for many of us, this
may be first time seeing it.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.129
So we'll learn something.

00:14:24.129 --> 00:14:25.920
Could we cut back over
to the demo, please?

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:31.670
Fantastic.

00:14:31.670 --> 00:14:34.150
First, let's just take a
quick look at the tool.

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:35.400
I'll give a quick walkthrough.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:38.160
And then we can look at
answering those questions.

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:40.730
So for starters,
we can get to this

00:14:40.730 --> 00:14:46.520
by going to the Billing tab
and the Reports section.

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:48.600
Right at the top,
here, of the page,

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:51.410
we have a summary bar, which
includes high-level information

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:54.590
about what our costs
are for our app.

00:14:54.590 --> 00:14:59.330
Below that, we have a chart that
lets us analyze trends and see

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:02.310
how our usage breaks down.

00:15:02.310 --> 00:15:04.430
And then at the
bottom, we have a table

00:15:04.430 --> 00:15:08.750
that summarizes and rolls up
the information from that chart.

00:15:08.750 --> 00:15:11.400
Lastly, on the right
section of the page,

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:13.070
we have grouping
and filter controls

00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:16.430
that let us slice and dice
our data and filter down

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:20.020
to the scope of spend that's
relevant for us to look at.

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:22.210
OK, so to answer
those questions,

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:24.810
first, what are our costs?

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:27.300
The summary bar gives us a
really nice, concise answer

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:27.990
to that.

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:31.950
So far, in the first 26
days of July, for our demo

00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:35.540
here, we've spent $1,400.

00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:37.279
For the entire month
of July, looking

00:15:37.279 --> 00:15:38.820
at the right half
of the summary bar,

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:42.999
we can see that we're forecasted
to spend about $1,800.

00:15:42.999 --> 00:15:44.790
So we can see easily
that the summary bar--

00:15:44.790 --> 00:15:46.940
it gives us a really
nice, quick answer

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:50.370
that we can answer that
question in maybe 5 seconds.

00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:53.800
Next, what are our cost trends?

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:55.740
In the first 26 days
of July, it looks

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:58.080
like our costs are
down about 3.5%

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:00.640
over the preceding
26-day period.

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:02.220
But for the entire
month of July,

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:04.770
it looks like we're
forecasted to spend about 8.5%

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:08.140
more than we did in
the month of June.

00:16:08.140 --> 00:16:10.320
This is because we're
growing, also because July

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:11.860
has one more day than June.

00:16:11.860 --> 00:16:16.010
So we would expect costs to
go up a little bit anyways.

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:18.430
But to look at these
trends in more detail,

00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:20.900
I'm going to look
down at the chart.

00:16:20.900 --> 00:16:24.530
A few things here to note-- one,
we have a stacked line chart.

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:27.320
So the total height
of the stacked line

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:30.290
is what we're spending
on a daily level.

00:16:30.290 --> 00:16:33.110
But each one of these segments
represents a meaningful portion

00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:35.407
of our spend.

00:16:35.407 --> 00:16:36.740
Here, we're grouping by project.

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:39.090
So each one of these
colored segments

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:42.560
represents the spend
for a single project.

00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:46.210
At Acme Company, we organize
our applications into projects.

00:16:46.210 --> 00:16:48.020
So each one of these
colored segments

00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:51.310
is how much we're spending on
one application for one day.

00:16:54.430 --> 00:16:57.310
Lastly, we have a
dotted gray line here

00:16:57.310 --> 00:17:00.254
that represents our cost trend.

00:17:00.254 --> 00:17:02.170
And this is how we get
to this forecasted cost

00:17:02.170 --> 00:17:03.385
number for the month.

00:17:03.385 --> 00:17:09.010
The forecasted cost is the sum
of our actual spend to date

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:11.890
and our forecasted spend for
the remainder of the month.

00:17:14.854 --> 00:17:15.791
But to look at it--

00:17:15.791 --> 00:17:18.290
I want to explain a little bit
how we get to this cost trend

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:18.789
number.

00:17:18.789 --> 00:17:21.800
It's a feature we've
rolled out recently.

00:17:21.800 --> 00:17:24.869
And it's pretty powerful.

00:17:24.869 --> 00:17:27.069
So I'm going to select the
custom date range here,

00:17:27.069 --> 00:17:28.985
just so that we can get
a little more context.

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:40.690
I'll look at the period
from May 1 to August 25.

00:17:40.690 --> 00:17:42.820
OK, so for the
purposes of the demo,

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:46.090
we created this account
sometime in mid-May.

00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:50.770
And the account grew along
with our hypothetical demand.

00:17:50.770 --> 00:17:54.790
And the cost trend picks up on
that long-term, secular, stable

00:17:54.790 --> 00:17:55.600
trend.

00:17:55.600 --> 00:17:59.060
And it projects it out up to
about a month into the future.

00:17:59.060 --> 00:18:01.090
So the way this
works is, we take

00:18:01.090 --> 00:18:03.017
the entire history for
the billing account,

00:18:03.017 --> 00:18:04.600
all the spend that
you've had to date,

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:08.030
and we look for the most
recent stable long-term trend.

00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:11.240
So maybe, if you launched a new
application, like Waze Carpool,

00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:12.580
it would start at that point.

00:18:12.580 --> 00:18:15.490
Or if you went through a major
cost optimization effort,

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:17.830
it might start after that.

00:18:17.830 --> 00:18:20.290
It also does a good job
of ignoring spikes like we

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:21.624
see here in the middle of June.

00:18:21.624 --> 00:18:24.040
So it really just picks up on
whatever your organic growth

00:18:24.040 --> 00:18:26.530
trend is and will forecast
that forward into the future.

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:34.430
Great.

00:18:34.430 --> 00:18:37.520
So now, we have a quick answer
of our first two questions,

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:42.060
what are my costs and
what are my cost trends?

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:45.080
But we've answered this only
for the entire billing account.

00:18:45.080 --> 00:18:46.788
What if we want to
know a little bit more

00:18:46.788 --> 00:18:49.437
about a single
application-- in this case,

00:18:49.437 --> 00:18:51.020
let's say Phill's
Amazing Application,

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:52.395
not to toot my
own horn too much.

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:59.590
We can answer the same
questions just by filtering down

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:01.130
to that one project.

00:19:01.130 --> 00:19:03.760
We can see that so
far, we've spent $450

00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:05.410
on the application this month.

00:19:05.410 --> 00:19:08.830
We're expected to spend about
$600 in the month of July.

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:13.600
And that represents about a 36%
growth over the month of June.

00:19:21.379 --> 00:19:21.879
OK.

00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:27.250
So lastly, the one
question we haven't really

00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:30.880
dove into too much yet is,
what are my cost drivers?

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:34.290
So to do that, I want to
go back to those spikes

00:19:34.290 --> 00:19:35.827
that we saw in June.

00:19:35.827 --> 00:19:37.410
I've selected the
previous month here,

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:41.070
so we can look at those
in a bit more detail.

00:19:41.070 --> 00:19:43.430
So here, we see a
spike around June 8.

00:19:43.430 --> 00:19:45.682
And we see another
spike around June 11.

00:19:45.682 --> 00:19:47.390
And I want to know,
at a high level, what

00:19:47.390 --> 00:19:49.907
changed to cause those spikes?

00:19:49.907 --> 00:19:51.990
The chart's a really
powerful tool for doing this.

00:19:51.990 --> 00:19:54.448
And we can quickly see, just
by hovering over this section,

00:19:54.448 --> 00:19:57.947
that this orange slice, which
was our Good Enough Project,

00:19:57.947 --> 00:19:59.030
that really changed a lot.

00:19:59.030 --> 00:20:00.540
It grew at that time.

00:20:00.540 --> 00:20:03.660
And it also grew for
the June 11 spike.

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:05.360
On that June 11
spike, we also see

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:08.210
that we had some growth in
this blue segment, which was

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:09.922
Phill's Amazing Application.

00:20:09.922 --> 00:20:11.380
But I can get a
little more detail.

00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:14.470
I want to understand what
changed in those applications

00:20:14.470 --> 00:20:16.385
to cause those spikes.

00:20:16.385 --> 00:20:19.497
And let's look at Good
Enough Project first.

00:20:19.497 --> 00:20:21.205
So I'll filter down
to just that project.

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:25.430
And then I want to
ask the question,

00:20:25.430 --> 00:20:27.620
which products or
services changed

00:20:27.620 --> 00:20:30.020
to cause the cost to increase?

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:32.300
So I'll group by product
instead of by project.

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:37.450
And now, I can see that
BigQuery, all of a sudden,

00:20:37.450 --> 00:20:41.140
went from nothing
to $28 overnight.

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:43.050
And that may have
been unexpected.

00:20:43.050 --> 00:20:45.010
It may have been
something we planned for.

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:46.840
We can get even a
little more detail

00:20:46.840 --> 00:20:49.810
and see what happened in
BigQuery by filtering down

00:20:49.810 --> 00:20:52.970
to just that
product and grouping

00:20:52.970 --> 00:20:55.379
by SKU to see if there
are any trends in the SKUs

00:20:55.379 --> 00:20:56.170
that we were using.

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:03.290
So not surprisingly, it's
analysis, not storage,

00:21:03.290 --> 00:21:07.820
that fluctuated really highly
up and then came back down.

00:21:07.820 --> 00:21:10.070
And at this point, this
is about as much detail

00:21:10.070 --> 00:21:11.440
as we can get from this report.

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:14.600
But we might want to go talk
to, say, our data science team

00:21:14.600 --> 00:21:16.880
and learn what kinds of
queries they were running.

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:18.410
And if they were
pulling a data set

00:21:18.410 --> 00:21:20.993
to build a new predictive model
that we get a lot of value out

00:21:20.993 --> 00:21:22.850
of, that may be totally fine.

00:21:22.850 --> 00:21:25.490
If, however, we
want to trade off

00:21:25.490 --> 00:21:28.040
some of the flexibility
for a little more

00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:29.810
predictable and
controllable costs,

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:31.550
we might want to
look into something

00:21:31.550 --> 00:21:33.200
like flat-rate BigQuery pricing.

00:21:37.038 --> 00:21:37.538
OK.

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:47.730
So now, I want to go
through a similar process

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:50.010
and try to understand what
happened in Phill's Amazing

00:21:50.010 --> 00:21:52.960
Application in the
middle of June.

00:21:52.960 --> 00:21:56.640
So again, I'm going to filter
down to just Phill's Amazing

00:21:56.640 --> 00:21:57.731
Application.

00:22:00.500 --> 00:22:02.960
And we can see, just by
hovering over the chart,

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:06.080
we were spending about $8 a day.

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:10.382
And then it jumped up to about
$35 a day sometime in mid-June.

00:22:10.382 --> 00:22:12.840
And that's something I'd want
to know-- say, what products,

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:15.130
what services changed.

00:22:15.130 --> 00:22:18.020
So again, I'll filter down
to just Compute Engine--

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:23.420
sorry, jumped ahead.

00:22:27.550 --> 00:22:29.270
I would group by product.

00:22:29.270 --> 00:22:31.550
And I would see what
products changed.

00:22:31.550 --> 00:22:34.490
In this case, we can see, in
Phill's Amazing Application,

00:22:34.490 --> 00:22:36.895
we run two products or
services, both App Engine

00:22:36.895 --> 00:22:38.650
and Compute Engine.

00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:40.830
And Compute Engine is
where the change occurred.

00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:42.000
And again, we can get
a little more detail

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:44.000
by looking at what our
changes were in our SKUs.

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:51.496
So here, I'll just filter down
to Compute Engine and group

00:22:51.496 --> 00:22:52.068
by SKU.

00:22:54.355 --> 00:22:55.980
And there's a couple
of changes that we

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:57.960
can see here during that spike.

00:22:57.960 --> 00:22:59.940
Normally, just by
hovering over, I can see,

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:03.150
we run n1-standard-1s, which is
our smallest standard machine

00:23:03.150 --> 00:23:05.940
type of the n1 family.

00:23:05.940 --> 00:23:09.060
But then, right here in
the middle of the month,

00:23:09.060 --> 00:23:11.010
we see a couple other
flavors of SKU--

00:23:11.010 --> 00:23:12.715
n1-standard-8s and
n1-standard-16s,

00:23:12.715 --> 00:23:15.090
which are bigger and more
expensive than what we normally

00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:15.945
run.

00:23:15.945 --> 00:23:17.570
And again, this is
about as much detail

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:19.500
as you can get from
a high-level report.

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:22.680
But you might want to dive
into more detailed data

00:23:22.680 --> 00:23:25.230
in, like, your Stackdriver
logs or in our BigQuery Billing

00:23:25.230 --> 00:23:25.747
Export.

00:23:25.747 --> 00:23:28.080
Or we might go talk to the
project owner or the engineer

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:30.780
and say, hey, is there a reason
why we're running these bigger

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:34.050
machines, and why
we didn't spin them

00:23:34.050 --> 00:23:36.723
down for a few days afterwards?

00:23:40.302 --> 00:23:41.760
Additionally, when
we see something

00:23:41.760 --> 00:23:44.190
like that, if it's
unexpected, we

00:23:44.190 --> 00:23:46.590
may want to set up a
passive monitoring solution,

00:23:46.590 --> 00:23:49.260
like Budgets and
Alerts, so that we're

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:52.350
notified if our spend trends
quicker than we expect.

00:23:52.350 --> 00:23:55.340
At this point, we can cut
back over to the slides.

00:23:55.340 --> 00:23:59.740
So now, we've gone through
a demo of Billing Reports.

00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:02.620
We've seen how we can use this
tool to really quickly get

00:24:02.620 --> 00:24:06.964
a high-level answer to our three
key cost management questions.

00:24:06.964 --> 00:24:08.380
And we've also
seen how we can get

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:10.810
to a certain level of
depth, at which point

00:24:10.810 --> 00:24:14.200
we need to go to either a
more detailed data source,

00:24:14.200 --> 00:24:17.500
like our Billing Export,
or like Stackdriver

00:24:17.500 --> 00:24:20.260
logs, to get even more rich
detail about, say, machine

00:24:20.260 --> 00:24:21.426
utilization.

00:24:21.426 --> 00:24:23.050
Or we can go talk to
the relevant teams

00:24:23.050 --> 00:24:25.090
to figure out what happened
when we see spikes,

00:24:25.090 --> 00:24:26.506
like we did in the
middle of June.

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:34.710
So that concludes our demo.

00:24:34.710 --> 00:24:37.230
Before we wrap up, I
just want to highlight

00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:38.700
a couple of other
sessions related

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:41.910
to cost management and billing.

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:45.120
On Tuesday, we had a
talk by James Byers

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:48.540
on how to organize and structure
your resources, which also

00:24:48.540 --> 00:24:50.160
relates to the
slide that Stephen

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:53.160
went through earlier about
labels versus projects

00:24:53.160 --> 00:24:55.830
and folders.

00:24:55.830 --> 00:24:58.740
We also had a pretty cool talk
yesterday with Dale and Dale

00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:01.290
from Vendasta, where they did
some interesting live demos

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:04.380
about how to use BigQuery
Export and Data Studio to slice

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:06.760
and dice your data.

00:25:06.760 --> 00:25:10.530
And lastly, Matt
and Lucas Carlson

00:25:10.530 --> 00:25:12.540
talked a bit about how
we can control our cloud

00:25:12.540 --> 00:25:15.460
costs with financial
governance tools.

00:25:15.460 --> 00:25:17.700
We also have a
showcase downstairs,

00:25:17.700 --> 00:25:19.560
on the bottom floor
of Moscone West.

00:25:19.560 --> 00:25:21.930
Please stop by and say hi.

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:24.360
We'll be there, probably
after this presentation.

